#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183858 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c872e855-895e-5369-8a79-cbdf21133548" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation Given a Corresponding Perfect Square"}] :question/id #uuid "9815dede-5c47-5430-a58a-218313f78412" :question/math-academy-id "e-10014" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-2x^2 - 4x = 1$.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $x^2 + 2x + 1$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\nx^2 + 2x + 1 = (x + 1)^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x^{2} - 4x &= 1 \\\\\n- 2(x^{2} + 2x) &= 1 \\\\\n- 2(x^{2} + 2 \\cdot 1x) &= 1 \\\\\n- 2(x^{2} + 2 \\cdot 1x + 1^{2} - 1^{2}) &= 1 \\\\\n- 2[(x + 1)^{2} - 1^{2}] &= 1 \\\\\n- 2[(x + 1)^{2} - 1] &= 1 \\\\\n- 2(x + 1)^{2} + (-2)(-1) &= 1 \\\\\n- 2(x + 1)^{2} + 2 &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by subtracting $2$ from both sides and then dividing by $(-2)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2(x + 1)^{2} + 2 &= 1 \\\\\n- 2(x + 1)^{2} + 2 - 2 &= 1 - 2 \\\\\n- 2(x + 1)^{2} &= -1 \\\\\n(x + 1)^{2} &= ((-1))/((-2)) \\\\\n(x + 1)^{2} &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 1)^{2} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n\\sqrt{(x + 1)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\n\\mid x + 1 \\mid &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\nx + 1 &= ± \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\nx &= -1 ± \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184027 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "257b907c-70c1-5bbd-a6d2-297d51325d1f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "6e12005d-4652-532c-a715-3ab9d9ad3618" :question/math-academy-id "e-7139" :question/problem "Write the equation $2x^2 - 6x = 1$ in the form $(x+p)^2 = q$, and hence solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we divide out by the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} - 6x &= 1 \\\\\n2(x^{2} - 3x) &= 1 \\\\\nx^{2} - 3x &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nx^{2} - 2 \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot x &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nx^{2} - 2 \\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}) \\cdot x + (\\frac{3}{2})^{2} - (\\frac{3}{2})^{2} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n(x - \\frac{3}{2})^{2} - (\\frac{3}{2})^{2} &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - \\frac{3}{2})^{2} - (\\frac{3}{2})^{2} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n(x - \\frac{3}{2})^{2} - \\frac{9}{4} &= \\frac{2}{4} \\\\\n(x - \\frac{3}{2})^{2} &= \\frac{11}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\np=-\\dfrac32\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nq=\\dfrac{11}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{(x - \\frac{3}{2})^{2}} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{4}} \\\\\n\\mid x - \\frac{3}{2} \\mid &= \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2} \\\\\nx - \\frac{3}{2} &= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{3}{2} ± \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{3 ± \\sqrt{11}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184028 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "9dfe0c3a-2c54-5270-ab0d-0d2dad09a199" :question/math-academy-id "e-7140" :question/problem "Write the equation $-2x^2+2x+1 = 0$ in the form $2(x+p)^2=q$, and hence solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-1) \\cdot (-2x^{2} + 2x + 1) &= (-1) \\cdot 0 \\\\\n2x^{2} - 2x - 1 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} - 2x - 1 &= 0 \\\\\n2x^{2} - 2x &= 1 \\\\\n2(x^{2} - x) &= 1 \\\\\n2[x^{2} - 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x + (\\frac{1}{2})^{2} - (\\frac{1}{2})^{2}] &= 1 \\\\\n2[(x - \\frac{1}{2})^{2} - \\frac{1}{4}] &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2[(x - \\frac{1}{2})^{2} - \\frac{1}{4}] &= 1 \\\\\n2(x - \\frac{1}{2})^{2} - \\frac{1}{2} &= 1 \\\\\n2(x - \\frac{1}{2})^{2} &= \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\np=-\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nq=\\dfrac32\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(x - \\frac{1}{2})^{2} &= \\frac{3}{2} \\\\\n(x - \\frac{1}{2})^{2} &= \\frac{3}{4} \\\\\n\\sqrt{(x - \\frac{1}{2})^{2}} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\\n\\mid x - \\frac{1}{2} \\mid &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\nx - \\frac{1}{2} &= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1}{2} ± \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "257b907c-70c1-5bbd-a6d2-297d51325d1f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82072ee0-13f5-5686-82b1-ae2000b3312f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q =-1, x = 1 ± \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183438} {:answer/id #uuid "92fce56f-5163-5f74-bfab-b2b7fd956c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q =-1, x = 1 ± \\sqrt{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183439} {:answer/id #uuid "c70de482-14f4-5806-bb6e-7e2b7620519b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q = 11, x =-1 ± \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183440} {:answer/id #uuid "0f68b0e9-0392-527a-bc03-1df0c43ae5f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q = 11, x = 1 ± \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183441} {:answer/id #uuid "3a33f08d-733e-5fc5-a89e-856cabe406e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q = 11, x = 1 ± \\sqrt{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f68b0e9-0392-527a-bc03-1df0c43ae5f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q = 11, x = 1 ± \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183441} :answer-field/id #uuid "9790c489-8408-5721-a9a9-6746e95691ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183437}] :question/id #uuid "404aac0a-c4f3-5720-8a82-6f8114d49cae" :question/math-academy-id "q-60360" :question/problem "The equation $2x^{2} - 4x - 9 = 0$ can be written in the form $2(x + p)^{2} = q$. Find $q$, and solve the equation."}]}
